            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/meta-clemente/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why are we changing settings as part of a leak?  \"Settings\" are generally intended to be user-interface, not programmatic."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/meta-clemente/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Kimi Chen",
                        "email": "kimi.zy.chen@fii-foxconn.com",
                        "username": "zuyan-kimi"
                    },
                    "message": "Please refer to item #20 for my response."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/meta-clemente/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 10,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Kimi Chen",
                        "email": "kimi.zy.chen@fii-foxconn.com",
                        "username": "zuyan-kimi"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/meta-clemente/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "It seems like we're doing this because of some subtle code in phosphor-logging.  How do we ever clear this?  Doesn't this mean that any leak will permanently make it so that any error powers off the host?  This doesn't seem like great behavior."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/meta-clemente/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Kimi Chen",
                        "email": "kimi.zy.chen@fii-foxconn.com",
                        "username": "zuyan-kimi"
                    },
                    "message": "Please refer to item #20 for my response."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/meta-clemente/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 11,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Kimi Chen",
                        "email": "kimi.zy.chen@fii-foxconn.com",
                        "username": "zuyan-kimi"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/meta-clemente/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 20,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Please use log-create"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/meta-clemente/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 20,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Kimi Chen",
                        "email": "kimi.zy.chen@fii-foxconn.com",
                        "username": "zuyan-kimi"
                    },
                    "message": "Hi Patrick,\n\nThanks for the review.\n\nAfter tracing the log-create usage, I found that it uses sdbusplus and phosphor-dbus-interface YAMLs to filter input parameters. We would need to add CALLOUT_ metadata to Detector.events.yaml to allow phosphor-logging to generate a blocking entry event.\n\nI am considering dropping the changes to critical-leak-handler entirely. Instead, I propose modifying leakdetector to generate the blocking entry event directly. This maintains the original event report content while allowing QuiesceOnHwError to prevent the Host from powering on.\n\nProposed Changes:\n1.\nhttps://github.com/openbmc/phosphor-dbus-interfaces/blob/012ab1d9c003bef49e972d81de3e2a69a2be1a1b/yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/State/Leak/Detector.events.yaml#L7\n->\n```diff\n-          - name: DetectorName\n+          - name: CalloutInventoryPath\n```\n\n2.\nhttps://github.com/openbmc/dbus-sensors/blob/88dfc5b88094b54d6443c2ec5154f8a1f3e70693/src/leakdetector/LeakEvents.cpp#L51\n->\n```diff\n-\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 error_intf::LeakDetectedCritical(\"DETECTOR_NAME\", detectorPath));\n+\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 error_intf::LeakDetectedCritical(\"CALLOUT_INVENTORY_PATH\",\n+\u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0 \u00a0detectorPath));\n```\n\nAdditionally, instead of modifying QuiesceOnHwError via the user interface, I plan to set the default value of QuiesceOnHwError to true specifically for the Clemente platform.\n\nWhat do you think about this approach?\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------\n\nBlocking the Power-On Process:\n1. The leakdetector reports a critical leak event.\n\n2. In phosphor-logging, the system checks the QuiesceOnHwError setting and verifies if additionalData contains CALLOUT_ to determine whether to create a block entry. If triggered, it executes obmc-host-graceful-quiesce@0.target.\n\n3. This prompts the host-state-manager to initiate an auto-reboot. This performs a power cycle (power off and power on), effectively preventing the host from completing the boot process while the error persists.\n\n4. Once the leakdetector reports a normal leak event (cleared status), it calls Resolve to remove the block entry. This unblocks the boot sequence, allowing the power-on process to resume and complete."
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/meta-clemente/recipes-phosphor/sensors/dbus-sensors/critical-leak-handler",
                    "line": 20,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Kimi Chen",
                        "email": "kimi.zy.chen@fii-foxconn.com",
                        "username": "zuyan-kimi"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/recipes-phosphor/state/phosphor-state-manager_%.bbappend",
                    "line": 117,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Why is this special for Clemente?  Shouldn't all platforms honor that?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/recipes-phosphor/state/phosphor-state-manager_%.bbappend",
                    "line": 117,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Kimi Chen",
                        "email": "kimi.zy.chen@fii-foxconn.com",
                        "username": "zuyan-kimi"
                    },
                    "message": "Understood. To clarify, are you suggesting that this dependency should be integrated directly into the base obmc-host-startmin@.target in phosphor-state-manager(https://github.com/openbmc/phosphor-state-manager/blob/master/target_files/obmc-host-start-pre%40.target) for all platforms to honor, rather than keeping it as a platform-specific .bbappend for Clemente?\n\nOr should I generalize the implementation within the current recipe but remove the platform-specific naming?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "meta-facebook/meta-catalina/recipes-phosphor/state/phosphor-state-manager_%.bbappend",
                    "line": 117,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Kimi Chen",
                        "email": "kimi.zy.chen@fii-foxconn.com",
                        "username": "zuyan-kimi"
                    },
                    "message": "Hi Patrick,\n\nFollowing our last discussion, I have moved the leakage logic to leakdetector. Regarding the logic for blocking the host power-on transition:\n\nWe originally discussed using gpio-monitor to monitor the FM_MAIN_PWREN_RMC_EN_ISO_R signal from the RMC and create a record flag file to prevent the Power State Manager (PSM) from starting the host.\nReference:\nhttps://github.com/openbmc/openbmc/blob/87bfd5b7c390e2d642d0808e33002b0be322f1ad/meta-facebook/meta-catalina/recipes-phosphor/gpio/phosphor-gpio-monitor/clemente/platform-gpio-monitor#L217\n\nHowever, I noticed that the current host-poweron@.service in the Clemente platform already includes logic to check the RMC signal via I2C to block the boot process. Therefore, I did not implement the additional record flag file.\nReference:\nhttps://github.com/openbmc/openbmc/blob/87bfd5b7c390e2d642d0808e33002b0be322f1ad/meta-facebook/meta-catalina/recipes-phosphor/state/phosphor-state-manager/clemente/power-cmd#L106\n\nThanks a lot."
                }
            ],
